<LegalDoc
  title="Cookie Policy"
  description="What cookies and similar technologies TaskForce uses, and how to control them. A working draft."
  eyebrow="Legal & Trust"
  updated="13 September 2026"
  draft={true}
>
  <p>
    This page explains the cookies and similar technologies TaskForce uses. We keep them to a minimum -
    we don't use cookies to track you across the web.
  </p>

  <h2>What we use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Strictly necessary</strong> - the application uses session and authentication tokens
      (through our identity provider) so you can sign in and stay signed in. The Service can't work
      without these, so they don't require consent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Preferences</strong> - small values that remember choices such as your theme, where you set
      them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Analytics</strong> - we use <strong>PostHog (EU-hosted)</strong> to understand which pages
      and features are used, so we can improve the product. It's <strong>off by default</strong> and
      loads <strong>only after you opt in</strong> through the cookie banner; it then sets a first-party
      identifier to count visits and page views. No advertising, no cross-site tracking. You can withdraw
      at any time via <strong>"Manage cookies"</strong> in the footer.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your choices</h2>
  <p>
    You can clear or block cookies in your browser settings. Blocking strictly necessary cookies will
    stop parts of the Service from working. Where we use anything beyond strictly necessary cookies,
    we'll ask for your consent first and let you change it at any time.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If our use of cookies changes, we'll update this page and the date above. Questions? See our
    <a href="/legal/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
</LegalDoc>
